fix(mcp,hooks): let a tool call name its session, so a refused start recovers (#226)

MEASURED. Runs in the ledger by agent type were 101 orchestrator, 69
general-purpose and 3 session. A subagent got an identity every time and the
operator's own session almost never did.

SessionStart was not broken -- driven against a running deployment it minted a
run at once. It runs ONCE, at the moment the stack is least likely to be up, it
is deliberately non-blocking, and nothing ever tried again. observe_tool_call
then required a run id, so a shim whose start was refused had no run id and
could not record tool calls either: the session lost its identity AND every call
it went on to make, silently, after one warning at the beginning. Orchestrator
identity hid it, because the signer registers a fresh run per commit.

The fix is not a retry in the shim. E11's rule is that anything a second harness
would have to copy belongs in the MCP, and a retry written in one shim is a
retry every future shim reimplements. So session_id is an alternative to run_id,
and a session this deployment has never seen is registered by the call that
names it -- through observe_session's own start, in process, so there is one
definition of starting a session and not two.

Three decisions rather than fallout. Naming both a run and a session that
disagree is refused, never a precedence order, because the answer is on its way
into an append-only record. A first-sight registration takes cwd and agent_type
for the same reason a start does, rather than registering against a guessed
repository. And a retired session is reported terminal and never resurrected --
#207 keeps the marker past retirement for exactly that, and a tool call naming
one is the same call in a different spelling.

THE SHIM CAUGHT A MISATTRIBUTION IN THIS CHANGE. There are two kinds of run: a
subagent's, keyed on the agent id, and the operator's session, keyed on the
session id. Passing the session id unconditionally attributed a subagent's tool
calls to the run of the session that spawned it. The selftest found it, and the
key is now derived once, next to the marker key it is easy to confuse with, with
two cases pinning both directions.

Driven by MCP-061..066, proposed in doc 07 first and observed failing. Two
existing cases changed with the contract and say why in place: the schema now
carries three more arguments, and an empty run_id with no session is no longer
RUN_NOT_FOUND but its own refusal, since a verdict about a run reads wrongly for
a caller who named none. IP §4 amended.

Proved end to end against the running stack, not asserted: a session with no
marker and nothing in the ledger made one tool call, and the chain then held
run_registered 1, tool_call 1.

